---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Related from "../components/Related.astro";
import { PINNED_KEY, IDENTITY } from "../lib/site.ts";

/**
 * AN ORDINARY PRIVACY POLICY. Deliberately short, deliberately unremarkable.
 *
 * THIS PAGE HAS NOW BEEN WRONG IN BOTH DIRECTIONS, which is the reason it is written the way
 * it is. The first version was an argument: "no analytics, no third-party scripts, no
 * off-origin requests", plus three paragraphs on a Cloudflare beacon. Then the site ran Google
 * Analytics 4 and those sentences became false, so the page was rewritten as a compliance
 * document. GA4 is now gone — see the note where its constants used to live in site.ts — and
 * the GA-shaped half of that rewrite is false in turn.
 *
 * The lesson is not "be vague". It is that this page must describe WHAT RUNS, checked, on the
 * day it is edited. So there are no gates left in it. There is no GA_ENABLED flag deciding
 * which of two paragraphs a reader sees, because that flag is what let the page describe a
 * configuration instead of a fact. Every claim below was measured against the live site.
 *
 * AND IT HAS NOW BEEN WRONG IN A THIRD WAY, which is why the Analytics section reads as it
 * does. It said "None run on this site", and that was true of the site's own code and true of
 * what a browser executed — but the sentence next to it, that Cloudflare's injected beacon
 * "does not run", was paired in the CSP comment with "the tag itself is being turned off at
 * the dashboard". That never happened. For eight days the page carried a script it could not
 * execute, the account recorded zero pageloads, and a comment in src/middleware.ts described
 * an intention in the present tense. The beacon is now permitted on purpose, and every claim
 * about what it does below is either quoted from Cloudflare with its URL or measured — the
 * cookie and query-string claims are measurements of the beacon's own source, which anybody
 * can repeat with curl and grep.
 *
 * THE ONE SUBTLE THING, and the reason the Cloudflare section is longer than a hosting note
 * has any right to be: Cloudflare injects a measurement beacon into HTML responses, and it
 * decides whether to do so from the request's `accept` HEADER. Isolated on the live site on 19
 * August, four ways: an HTML accept gets the tag and 359 extra bytes; a wildcard accept does
 * not; the user-agent changes nothing in either direction. The first pass at this note said it
 * keyed off the user-agent, which was a guess dressed as a measurement — the difference was
 * sitting in a header that had not been varied. scripts/verify-live.mjs had the correct
 * explanation written at the top of it the whole time.
 *
 * It matters because a check that fetches this page without a browser's accept header sees a
 * clean document and reports one. verify-live sends a real browser's and therefore does see
 * it; the ad-hoc curl that produced the page-weight baseline did not, and reported zero. A
 * page describing what a reader receives has to be written from a request shaped like a
 * reader's.
 */
const title = "Privacy";
const description =
  "What Coinliqui collects: cookieless page-view analytics that reads no query string, one " +
  "preference cookie, and no payment or personal information. Every claim here is checkable.";
const updated = "27 August 2026";
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <div class="page-head">
    <h1>Privacy</h1>
    <p>What this site collects, and what it does not. Last updated {updated}.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 class="section">What we do not take</h2>
  <p class="prose">
    No payment details — nothing on this site is for sale and there is no checkout. No personal
    information: no name, email address, password, wallet connection or exchange API key is
    asked for anywhere. There are no accounts today, so there is nothing to register for and
    nothing to sign into. If that changes, this page changes with it.
  </p>

  <h2 class="section">Analytics</h2>
  <p class="prose">
    <strong>One counter runs: Cloudflare Web Analytics</strong>, permitted here since
    27&nbsp;August&nbsp;2026. It is a script Cloudflare injects into this site's pages at its
    edge; it was blocked by this site's Content-Security-Policy until that date and is now
    allowed by it, which is the only change. It exists to answer one question — whether anybody
    reads these pages — which nothing else here could answer.
  </p>
  <p class="prose">
    What it sends: the page's origin and <strong>path</strong>, the referring page if your
    browser supplies one, and page-load timings taken from the browser's own Performance API.
    Cloudflare states that it "does not collect or use your visitors' personal data" and that
    it "does not track individual end users across our customers' Internet properties" —
    <a class="link" href="https://developers.cloudflare.com/web-analytics/" rel="nofollow noopener">their documentation</a>.
  </p>
  <p class="prose">
    Those are their words. These are measurements, made on 27&nbsp;August&nbsp;2026 and
    repeatable by anyone:
  </p>
  <div class="panel" style="max-width:860px">
    <table class="tbl">
      <thead><tr><th>Claim</th><th>How it was checked, and how you can</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>It sets no cookie and no browser storage</td>
          <td class="reason">The beacon's source contains no <code>document.cookie</code>, no <code>localStorage</code>, no <code>sessionStorage</code> and no <code>indexedDB</code> — fetch the script named in this page's markup and search it. Loaded on a live site running the same beacon, the page ended with zero cookies and zero storage keys.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>It does not send the query string</td>
          <td class="reason">The beacon reads <code>location.pathname</code> and <code>location.origin</code> and rebuilds the URL from those two. It never reads <code>location.href</code> or <code>location.search</code>; neither string appears in the file at all. This is the reason the calculators below are unaffected by it.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>It does not fingerprint</td>
          <td class="reason">No canvas, no audio context, no device enumeration in the source. What it reads is the Performance API, which reports how long the page took to load and nothing about you.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>The data goes to this domain, not a third one</td>
          <td class="reason">The measurement is posted to <code>/cdn-cgi/rum</code> on this origin. That is why this site's <code>connect-src</code> is still <code>'self'</code> — no off-origin host is permitted to receive a request from any page here.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="section-note" style="margin-top:10px">
    The one host now permitted to serve a script here is <code>static.cloudflareinsights.com</code>,
    and it is the only one — you can read the whole policy in this page's response headers.
  </p>
  <p class="prose">
    <strong>Google Analytics 4 is gone and is not coming back.</strong> It was installed here
    between 17 and 19&nbsp;August&nbsp;2026 and removed: no tag, no <code>gtag.js</code>, no
    <code>_ga</code> cookie, and no request to any Google host from any page. It was dropped
    because it was never used — not one decision about this site was ever made from a GA4
    number — and it cost 167.8&nbsp;KB of JavaScript to answer questions nobody was asking, on
    a homepage of about 6.5&nbsp;KB. The counter that replaced it is 11.4&nbsp;KB over the wire,
    sets nothing, and answers one question that is actually asked.
  </p>

  <h2 class="section">Cookies and local storage</h2>
  <div class="panel" style="max-width:860px">
    <table class="tbl">
      <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Kind</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Expires</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><code>rail</code></td>
          <td>Cookie</td>
          <td>Whether you collapsed the sidebar. Two possible values; not an identifier</td>
          <td>1 year</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          {/* `set:text` RATHER THAN `<code>{PINNED_KEY}</code>`, and it is not a style choice.
              An expression as the only child of an inline element inside a table cell makes
              @astrojs/compiler emit two phantom `<code>` elements, one of them left open after
              the enclosing `</div>` — which put every element from here to the end of this page
              in monospace, on the live site, until 19 August. scripts/checks.mjs
              phantomInlineElements now fails the build on it. Renders identically. */}
          <td><code set:text={PINNED_KEY} /></td>
          <td>Local storage</td>
          <td>The coins you pinned on <a href="/watchlist">your watchlist</a>. Never sent anywhere</td>
          <td>Until you clear it</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="section-note" style="margin-top:10px">
    Two entries, and the analytics above adds neither a third nor anything else — see the
    measurement in that section. Clearing your browser data removes both of these and the site
    behaves the same without them.
  </p>

  <h2 class="section">The calculators</h2>
  <p class="prose">
    The <a href="/tools">calculators</a> compute in the page and store nothing. Their forms
    submit as <code>GET</code>, so the values you enter appear in the page's own URL — which is
    what makes a result shareable, and also means those numbers reach ordinary web-server
    request logs. Nothing links them to you, but if you would rather a real account balance did
    not appear in a log line, use a representative figure.
  </p>
  <p class="prose">
    Those request logs are still the only place the values go, and the analytics above does not
    change that: the beacon rebuilds the URL from the origin and the path, so a figure typed
    into a field named <code>capital</code> is not part of what it sends. That is a measurement
    of the script, stated in the table above, not an assurance.
  </p>
  <p class="prose">
    It was not always true. While Google Analytics ran, the full URL of every page view — query
    string included — was sent to Google as part of the standard page-view event. That was true
    from the day the tag went in, it was not described on this page, and it stopped when the tag
    came out.
  </p>

  <h2 class="section">Hosting</h2>
  <p class="prose">
    This site runs on Cloudflare. Serving a page means Cloudflare handles the request itself —
    your IP address, the page asked for, the browser string, the response status — and keeps
    aggregate traffic and security metrics from it. That much is true of any web host and is not
    optional for a site that is served at all.
  </p>
  <p class="prose">
    The measurement script described above is Cloudflare's too, and it is <strong>injected by
    the host rather than written into this site's source</strong>: it appears in the HTML after
    this site's code has finished with the response, and it does so whenever a client asks for
    the page the way a browser asks for it. It was blocked here until 27&nbsp;August&nbsp;2026
    and is permitted now. Nothing else off-origin can serve a script, receive a fetch, or load
    an image on any page here.
  </p>

  <h2 class="section">Contact</h2>
  <p class="prose">
    Questions about any of this, including a request to delete data associated with your
    visits: <!--email_off--><a class="link" href={`mailto:${IDENTITY.contact}`}>{IDENTITY.contact}</a><!--email_on-->.
  </p>

  <Related items={[
    { href: "/about", label: "About", note: "Who operates this site and what it is for." },
    { href: "/data-sources", label: "Data sources", note: "Every endpoint behind every number here." },
    { href: "/methodology", label: "Methodology", note: "How the numbers are produced." },
    { href: "/watchlist", label: "Watchlist", note: "The one feature that stores anything, and it stores it in your browser." },
  ]} />

  <style>
    h2.section { margin-top: 32px; }
  </style>
</Base>
